Alter the original artwork by replacing the oil painting process with a drypoint print on heavy etching paper, emphasizing dramatic tonal contrast, delicate linear burr textures, intricate organic shapes, and a strong focal point of emphasis on the glowing moon. Instead of a linen canvas, linseed oil, and heavy impasto paint applied with a palette knife, use a sharp steel drypoint needle to scratch directly into a copper or zinc plate, raising fine metal burrs that catch and hold rich etching ink during the wiping and intaglio printing process. Replace the techniques of slow-drying color blending, glazing, and impasto with traditional plate incising and selective ink wiping to highlight the fine, velvety linear burr textures across the gnarled tree bark, sky, and surrounding forest. The composition maintains the same core visual scene featuring a colossal, ancient gnarled tree at midnight illuminated by a brilliant full moon, surrounded by a dense, mysterious forest.
